        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is cleanly additive and lands in the right place:\n\n- `toDisplay` belongs in `OwnerTelephone`, which the class javadoc declares the \"single source of truth for the E.164 telephone concept.\" Formatting a stored E.164 value for humans is exactly that concept's responsibility, so the logic is in the correct unit rather than leaking into the mapper or DTO.\n- The MapStruct wiring (`@Mapping(... expression = \"java(OwnerTelephone.toDisplay(owner.getTelephone()))\")`) mirrors the existing `identityKey`/`contactPreference`/`ageBand` derived-field mappings exactly \u2014 idiomatic with its neighbors.\n- The OpenAPI `telephoneDisplay` entry matches the shape and tone of the surrounding `readOnly` derived properties.\n- The method is a self-contained static helper alongside `toE164`/`nationalLengthValid`, with javadoc consistent in style with the rest of the class.\n\nOne minor observation (not blocking): the known-country-code prefix knowledge (`startsWith(\"61\")` / `startsWith(\"1\")`) now appears in three spots \u2014 `toE164`'s implicit `61` default, `nationalLengthValid`, and `toDisplay`. A shared `countryCodeOf(digits)` helper would consolidate that, but the inline `startsWith` style already matches `nationalLengthValid`, so `toDisplay` follows the established local idiom rather than introducing a new one \u2014 a maintainer would find it natural, and extracting an abstraction over two country codes would be premature here."
      }
    ]
